{
  "text": "Earlier work showed that leaves of a common evergreen shrub display a strong daily pattern in apparent photosynthetic uptake when the concentration of the photosynthetic gas is low. Even though light intensity stays high, the uptake drops to very low levels at night and rises again in the morning. When the gas concentration is high enough that light, rather than the gas, limits the process, this daily pattern disappears and the light‑limited uptake remains steady for more than a day. In addition to the daily cycle, a seasonal trend is observed: the overall level of uptake is higher in late autumn than in midsummer months. For a given limiting gas concentration, uptake is greater under strong light than under weak light. The hypothesis that these changes are mainly due to variations in leaf pore opening was tested by comparing measurements from an instrument that records pore opening with simultaneous uptake rates, aiming to clarify the link between pore opening and photosynthetic performance."
